var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.add_histograms-Tuple{Vector{<:StatsBase.Histogram}}","page":"API","text":"add_histograms(hs::Vector{<:Histogram})\n\nSum the weights of histograms that share the same binning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.add_histograms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.build_forward_model-Tuple{AbstractDict{Symbol, <:StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}}","page":"API","text":"build_forward_model(pdfs; e_low, e_high, blind, norm, binning)\n\nBuild a function that maps fit parameters to the predicted counts per energy bin.\n\npdfs is a dictionary Dict(name => Histogram) of simulated 1D energy spectra that all share the same binning. Energies are in keV. Only bins fully inside [e_low, e_high] and outside the blind = (min, max) region are kept. binning merges that many consecutive bins first.\n\nReturns (forward_model, bin_edges):\n\nbin_edges: (low, high), the lower and upper edges in keV of the kept bins\nforward_model(params): predicted counts per kept bin for params = (name1 = a1, name2 = a2, ...), with one activity per spectrum, in any order. Each spectrum is scaled by activity * norm. Use norm for unit conversions and the livetime. Each call allocates one array.\n\nThe loop over spectra is unrolled at compile time, which only works for about 30 spectra or fewer. With more, each call makes extra small allocations and is slower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.build_forward_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.build_likelihood-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Function}","page":"API","text":"build_likelihood(counts, model)\n\nBuild the Poisson log-likelihood of the measured counts as a function of the parameters of model.\n\ncounts is a flat vector with one entry per bin, in the same order as the output of model(params). Get it from a histogram with get_counts, so that the bins are the ones the model predicts. model comes from build_forward_model.\n\nReturns a log-density function: evaluate it with DensityInterface.logdensityof(likelihood, params).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.build_likelihood"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.build_prior_dist-Tuple{AbstractDict{Symbol}}","page":"API","text":"build_prior_dist(ranges)\n\nBuild a prior distribution from the entries of get_priors, a Dict(name => prior). Each prior is either a range (Interval), which becomes a uniform distribution, or any 1D distribution from Distributions.jl, which is used as is. It is a NamedTupleDist (from ValueShapes.jl) and rand(prior) draws a named tuple of activities, which can be passed to the forward model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.build_prior_dist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.counts_to_histogram-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, NamedTuple{(:low, :high)}}","page":"API","text":"counts_to_histogram(counts, bin_edges)\n\nTurn a flat vector of counts back into a Histogram, for example to plot the model prediction. This is the inverse of get_counts.\n\ncounts[i] is the content of the bin between bin_edges.low[i] and bin_edges.high[i]. The bins must be sorted and must not overlap. The histogram covers the range from low[1] to high[end]. A gap between two bins, such as a blinded region, becomes one bin with 0 counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.counts_to_histogram"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.fit_axes-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"fit_axes(; scale = :log, title = \"\", ylabel = \"Counts\", size = (1300, 600), colors)\n\nMake a CairoMakie figure for the plot of a fit: an axis for the spectra with an axis for the residuals below it. The energy axes are linked and only the lower one has labels. Returns (fig, ax, ax_residuals).\n\nsize is the size of the figure in pixels. With scale = :log the y axis of ax is logarithmic, with :linear it is linear. The lines drawn by plot_hist! without a color take the next of colors, a vector of colors that repeats after the last one. The default is a color-blind friendly palette of 7 colors.\n\nDraw on the axes with plot_hist!, mark_blinded! and plot_residuals!, and set the limits, for example with CairoMakie.xlims!(ax, 100, 4000).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.fit_axes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.get_best_fit_histograms-Tuple{NamedTuple, Function, NamedTuple{(:low, :high)}}","page":"API","text":"get_best_fit_histograms(parameters, model, bin_edges)\n\nHistograms of the prediction of the model for the parameters (a named tuple of activities, for example the best fit). model and bin_edges come from build_forward_model.\n\nReturns an ordered dictionary name => Histogram with the total prediction in :total, followed by the contribution of each source under the name of its activity, in the order of parameters. The blinded region and the range outside of the fit are empty bins (see counts_to_histogram).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.get_best_fit_histograms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.get_counts-Tuple{StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}, NamedTuple{(:low, :high)}}","page":"API","text":"get_counts(data::Histogram, bin_edges)\n\nMeasured counts in the bins of the forward model, as a flat Vector{Float64}.\n\nbin_edges = (low = ..., high = ...) are the lower and upper edges of the bins, for example the ones returned by build_forward_model. The result is not a histogram: it has one entry per bin, in the same order as bin_edges. Each entry is the sum of the data bins between low[i] and high[i], which must be edges of data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.get_counts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.get_max_norm-Tuple{Real, Real, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}","page":"API","text":"get_max_norm(low, high, data, pdf; blind = nothing)\n\nLargest factor by which the simulated spectrum pdf can be scaled without exceeding the measured spectrum data: the ratio of the counts of data and pdf between low and high (keV). Only bins fully inside that range and outside the blind range (min, max) are used. data and pdf must have the same binning.\n\nReturns Inf if pdf has no counts in the range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.get_max_norm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.get_priors-Tuple{AbstractDict{Symbol, <:StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}","page":"API","text":"get_priors(pdfs, data; ref_ranges, e_low, e_high, blind, norm,\n           range_factor, peak_factor)\n\nEstimate a range 0 .. a_max for the activity of each simulated spectrum in pdfs (a Dict(name => Histogram)), based on the measured spectrum data. Returns Dict(name => Interval). The entries can be replaced by any 1D distribution from Distributions.jl before calling build_prior_dist.\n\nThese are not real informative priors. They come from the data and only limit the range of the fit to improve convergence. We can also use external knowledge for informative priors.\n\na_max is the largest activity for which the prediction stays below the data, in a window: a safety factor times get_max_norm divided by norm. It is the smaller of two values:\n\nthe value in the fit range [e_low, e_high], times range_factor (default 1.1),\nthe value in the reference range of the source, times peak_factor (default 1.5).\n\nref_ranges is a Dict(key => (low, high)) with energy ranges in keV, for example around a peak. A spectrum uses the range of the longest key that its name starts with, for example :K40_hpge_bulk uses :K40. Spectra without a range only use the fit range. e_low, e_high, blind and norm are as in build_forward_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.get_priors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Refit.get_valid_bins","page":"API","text":"get_valid_bins(edges, range, blind = nothing)\n\nBoolean mask of bins fully inside range = (min, max) and, if blind = (min, max) is given, fully outside the blinded region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.get_valid_bins"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.group_histograms-Tuple{AbstractDict{Symbol, <:StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}, AbstractVector{<:Pair}}","page":"API","text":"group_histograms(hists, groups)\n\nSum the sources of the best fit into groups, for example to plot fewer lines. hists is the result of get_best_fit_histograms and groups is a vector with one label => selection for each group. The selection is one of\n\na vector of source names, for example [:K40_cables, :K40_fibers],\na regular expression that is matched to the source names, for example r\"K40\",\n:rest, for all the sources that no other group selects.\n\nReturns an ordered dictionary in the same format as hists, with :total followed by the groups, which are named by their label as a Symbol. The label can be any text, including Unicode such as \"²¹²Pb\" or \"2νββ\". A source goes into the first group that selects it, and a group without sources is left out. A name that does not exist, or a regular expression that matches no source, is an error.\n\ngrouped = group_histograms(\n    hists,\n    [\"K40\" => r\"K40\", \"Pb212\" => [:Pb212_cables], \"other\" => :rest],\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.group_histograms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.integrate_hist-Tuple{StatsBase.Histogram{<:Any, 1}, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"integrate_hist(h::Histogram{<:Any,1}, a, b)\n\nTotal counts (sum of weights) of a 1D histogram between a and b, which must both lie on bin edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.integrate_hist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.mark_blinded!-Tuple{Any, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}","page":"API","text":"mark_blinded!(ax, h; kwargs...)\n\nMark the empty bins of the Histogram h on the CairoMakie axis ax with a vertical band. These are the bins that are not fitted, for example the blinded region in the histograms of get_best_fit_histograms. The keyword arguments, for example color, go to CairoMakie.vspan!, and the default is a grey band.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.mark_blinded!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.normalised_poisson_residual-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"normalised_poisson_residual(mu, obs)\n\nResidual of the observed counts obs with respect to the predicted counts mu, in units of standard deviations. Takes two numbers and returns a Float64, or two arrays of the same size and returns a Vector{Float64}.\n\nFor mu >= 50 the Poisson distribution is close to a Gaussian and the residual is (obs - mu) / sqrt(mu). For smaller mu it is the p-value of obs (the probability of at least obs counts above the mode, or of at most obs counts below it), converted into the number of standard deviations of a Gaussian. The sign is positive for an excess of data. A bin with obs < 1 and a prediction below 1 has residual 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.normalised_poisson_residual"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.plot_hist!-Tuple{Any, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}","page":"API","text":"plot_hist!(ax, h; filled = false, kwargs...)\n\nDraw the Histogram h as a step histogram on the CairoMakie axis ax: a line (with linewidth = 2 unless set), or a filled area with filled = true. The other keyword arguments, for example color, linewidth or label, go to the CairoMakie function that draws it (lines! or band!). Empty bins are drawn at 0, or at a very small value on a logarithmic axis. Returns the plot object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.plot_hist!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.plot_residuals!-Tuple{Any, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}, StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}","page":"API","text":"plot_residuals!(ax, prediction, data; bands = true, kwargs...)\n\nDraw the residuals of the data with respect to the prediction (both Histograms with the same bins) as points on the CairoMakie axis ax, in units of standard deviations, see normalised_poisson_residual. Bins where the prediction is empty are not fitted and are left out. With bands = true the bands of 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations are drawn behind the points. The keyword arguments, for example color or markersize, go to CairoMakie.scatter!. Returns the plot object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.plot_residuals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.poisson_likelihood-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"poisson_likelihood(obs, pred)\n\nPoisson log-likelihood of observed counts obs given predicted counts pred, dropping the constant -log(k!) term. pred must be positive; a tiny eps() is added to avoid log(0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.poisson_likelihood"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.read_hist-Tuple{String, String}","page":"API","text":" read_hist(group, file)\n\nRead a histogram in group group from an lh5 file file and return a Histogram object.\n\nThe histogram format is described in the LH5 specification. The group should contain a binning dataset and a weights dataset. Histograms written from Julia (for example by write_best_fit) are read too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.read_hist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.read_pdf-Tuple{AbstractDict{String, <:Real}}","page":"API","text":"read_pdf(files::AbstractDict; groups = \"pdf/mul_surv\", n_prim_field = \"nr_sim_events\")\n\nRead the same source simulated in several files, for example once per detector type, and return the weighted average of the spectra.\n\nfiles maps each file name to its weight, for example the mass of the detector type. The weights are divided by their sum. Each file is read with the single-file method, so it is divided by its own number of primaries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.read_pdf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.read_pdf-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"read_pdf(file; groups = \"pdf/mul_surv\", weights = nothing, n_prim_field = \"nr_sim_events\")\n\nRead a simulated spectrum from file, by default as counts per simulated primary.\n\ngroups is the name of one group, or a vector of names. The histograms in these groups are multiplied by weights (a vector with one factor per group, by default 1) and summed, then divided by the number of simulated primaries stored in the field n_prim_field. With n_prim_field = nothing the sum is not divided. To scale by anything else, change the weights of the result: h.weights ./= factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.read_pdf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.read_scalar-Tuple{String, String}","page":"API","text":"read_scalar(field, file)\n\nRead a Scalar from file called field\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.read_scalar"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.rebin_hist-Tuple{StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}, Int64}","page":"API","text":"rebin_hist(h::Histogram, factor::Int)\n\nRebins a Histogram by combining every factor consecutive bins of a 1D histogram. If the number of bins is not divisible by factor, the last new bin is narrower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.rebin_hist"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.write_best_fit-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractDict{Symbol, <:StatsBase.Histogram{<:Real, 1}}}","page":"API","text":"write_best_fit(file, hists; data = nothing)\n\nWrite the histograms of get_best_fit_histograms to the lh5 file file: the total in best_fit/total and each source in best_fit/<name>. An existing file is overwritten.\n\nWith data (a Histogram, for example from counts_to_histogram) the measured spectrum is also written, in data. The histograms can be read back with read_hist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.write_best_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Refit.write_parameter_values-Tuple{AbstractString, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"API","text":"write_parameter_values(file, parameters, intervals; level = 0.68)\n\nWrite the values of the parameters, for example the activities, to the YAML file file. For each parameter it saves the best fit value from parameters and the interval low to high from intervals. Both are named tuples with the same names, and each interval is an Interval.\n\nThe intervals should be the smallest credible intervals, the shortest intervals that contain the probability level of the posterior. BAT.jl computes them: map(first, BAT.smallest_credible_intervals(samples; p = 0.68, mode = :connected)). The level is saved in the file as credible_level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Refit.write_parameter_values"},{"category":"section","location":"likelihood/#Likelihood","page":"Likelihood","text":"poisson_likelihood compares measured and predicted counts in each bin. The counts in a bin follow a Poisson distribution with the predicted counts lambda_i as mean, so for measured counts k_i the log-likelihood is\n\nln L = sum_i left( k_i ln lambda_i - lambda_i right)\n\nThe constant term -ln k_i is left out. It does not depend on the parameters, so it does not change the position of the maximum or the shape of the posterior.","title":"Likelihood"},{"category":"section","location":"likelihood/#Measured-counts","page":"Likelihood","text":"The likelihood compares two flat vectors of the same length: the measured counts and the prediction of the forward model. Entry i of both is the number of counts in the same energy bin. The measured counts are not a histogram.\n\nget_counts extracts them from the data histogram data. It takes the lower and upper edge of each bin, in keV:\n\n# 100 keV bins between 500 and 3200 keV\nlow = collect(500.0:100.0:3100.0)  # lower edge of each bin\nbin_edges = (low = low, high = low .+ 100.0);  # upper edge = lower edge + 100 keV\n\n# measured counts in each bin, as a Vector{Float64}\ncounts = get_counts(data, bin_edges)\n27-element Vector{Float64}:\n 15601.0\n 14478.0\n 13478.0\n 12387.0\n 11268.0\n 10164.0\n  9107.0\n  8114.0\n  7196.0\n  9573.0\n     ⋮\n  1878.0\n  1631.0\n  1419.0\n  1837.0\n  1069.0\n   928.0\n   808.0\n   705.0\n   611.0\n\nThe bins can be any bins whose edges are also edges of data, otherwise an error is raised. A bin can span several bins of data, which are then summed.\n\nThe bin edges can also come from the forward model. build_forward_model returns the edges of the bins it predicts, whatever energy range, blinding and binning it uses. Using them guarantees that the measured and predicted bins match:\n\n# merge pairs of bins (20 keV bins) and keep 500 to 3200 keV\nmodel, bin_edges = build_forward_model(\n    mc_dict;\n    e_low = 500,\n    e_high = 3200,\n    binning = 2,\n    norm = 1e-3 * livetime,\n);\n\n# the measured counts in exactly the bins of the model\ncounts = get_counts(data, bin_edges)\ncounts\n135-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3112.0\n 3088.0\n 3062.0\n 3119.0\n 3220.0\n 2969.0\n 2933.0\n 2897.0\n 2859.0\n 2820.0\n    ⋮\n  145.0\n  141.0\n  137.0\n  133.0\n  129.0\n  125.0\n  123.0\n  119.0\n  115.0","title":"Measured counts"},{"category":"section","location":"likelihood/#Building-the-likelihood","page":"Likelihood","text":"using DensityInterface\n\n# the likelihood of the measured counts as a function of the parameters\nlikelihood = build_likelihood(counts, model);\n\nparams = (\n    K40_hpge_bulk = 40.0,\n    Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk = 10.0,\n    two_vbb_hpge_bulk = 100.0,\n)\n\n# log-likelihood for these parameters\nlogdensityof(likelihood, params)\n966590.9215598684\n\nThe value is largest when the prediction matches the data. Here the data were made with these activities, so changing one of them lowers the value:\n\nother = merge(params, (K40_hpge_bulk = 50.0,))\nlogdensityof(likelihood, other)\n966346.9409654519\n\nbuild_likelihood handles one measured spectrum and one model. To fit several spectra together, add the log-likelihoods of each one.\n\npoisson_likelihood(counts, pred) is the underlying function. It raises a DimensionMismatch if the two vectors differ in length. A bin with a predicted count of zero is allowed: a tiny number (eps()) is added to the prediction inside the logarithm, so the result stays finite.","title":"Building the likelihood"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Reading-simulated-spectra","page":"Reading spectra","text":"The simulated spectra are stored in LH5 files. read_hist(group, file) reads one histogram from the group group of file.\n\nThe examples below use a small file with two histograms and the number of simulated primaries (nr_sim_events), written here from a test file:\n\n# one raw histogram from the group \"pdf/mul_surv\"\nh = read_hist(\"pdf/mul_surv\", file)\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}\nedges:\n  0.0:10.0:1000.0\nweights: [177.0, 214.0, 220.0, 206.0, 212.0, 223.0, 208.0, 214.0, 211.0, 236.0  …  7.0, 11.0, 13.0, 11.0, 5.0, 12.0, 11.0, 6.0, 12.0, 13.0]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false","title":"Reading simulated spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Counts-per-simulated-primary","page":"Reading spectra","text":"A simulated spectrum is normally used as counts per simulated primary, so that the activity of the source scales it to the measured counts (see the forward model). read_pdf reads the histogram and divides it by the number of primaries in the field nr_sim_events:\n\n# the default group, divided by the number of primaries\npdf = read_pdf(file)\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}\nedges:\n  0.0:10.0:1000.0\nweights: [0.177, 0.214, 0.22, 0.206, 0.212, 0.223, 0.208, 0.214, 0.211, 0.236  …  0.007, 0.011, 0.013, 0.011, 0.005, 0.012, 0.011, 0.006, 0.012, 0.013]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false\n\ngroups is the name of the group to read, \"pdf/mul_surv\" by default. A vector of names reads several groups, which are summed before the division. n_prim_field is the name of the field with the number of primaries. With n_prim_field = nothing the histogram is not divided, and the result is the same as from read_hist.\n\n# sum two groups, then divide by the number of primaries\npdf = read_pdf(file; groups = [\"pdf/mul_surv\", \"pdf/mul_other\"])\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}\nedges:\n  0.0:10.0:1000.0\nweights: [0.531, 0.642, 0.66, 0.618, 0.636, 0.669, 0.624, 0.642, 0.633, 0.708  …  0.021, 0.033, 0.039, 0.033, 0.015, 0.036, 0.033, 0.018, 0.036, 0.039]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false","title":"Counts per simulated primary"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Weighting-the-groups","page":"Reading spectra","text":"weights gives one factor for each group, for example the fraction of the decays in each component. The groups are multiplied by their weight before they are summed:\n\npdf = read_pdf(file; groups = [\"pdf/mul_surv\", \"pdf/mul_other\"], weights = [0.7, 0.3])\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}\nedges:\n  0.0:10.0:1000.0\nweights: [0.2301, 0.2782, 0.286, 0.2677999999999999, 0.27559999999999996, 0.2899, 0.2704, 0.2782, 0.27429999999999993, 0.30679999999999996  …  0.0091, 0.014299999999999998, 0.0169, 0.014299999999999998, 0.0065, 0.015599999999999998, 0.014299999999999998, 0.007799999999999999, 0.015599999999999998, 0.0169]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false\n\nAny other factor can be applied to the result, which is a normal Histogram. For example, to divide by 2 in place:\n\npdf.weights ./= 2","title":"Weighting the groups"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#The-same-source-in-several-files","page":"Reading spectra","text":"A source is often simulated once per detector type. read_pdf takes a dictionary from file name to weight, for example the mass of each detector type, and returns the weighted average of the spectra. The weights are divided by their sum, and each file is divided by its own number of primaries.\n\nfiles = Dict(\n    \"icpc_bulk_2vbb-tier_pdf.lh5\" => 87.1,  # detector mass in kg\n    \"bege_bulk_2vbb-tier_pdf.lh5\" => 19.1,\n)\npdf = read_pdf(files; groups = \"pdf/mul_surv\")","title":"The same source in several files"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"The spectra, the best fit and its sources are plotted with CairoMakie, by the functions of this package. The posterior distributions are plotted with the plot recipes of BAT.jl, which use Plots.jl. The tutorial shows both for a complete fit.\n\nLoading LegendMakie.jl changes all CairoMakie plots to the LEGEND style. It is not needed, and it also needs Format to be loaded:\n\nusing LegendMakie\nimport Format","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Histograms-of-the-best-fit","page":"Plotting","text":"The plots compare the measured spectrum with the prediction of the best fit as histograms in the bins of the fit. counts_to_histogram turns the measured counts into a histogram, and get_best_fit_histograms makes the histograms of the prediction for the parameters best (for example mode(samples)): the total in the key :total, followed by the contribution of each source. The bins outside of the fit range and in the blinded region are empty.\n\nThe made-up data of this example has no statistical fluctuation, so each bin is drawn from a Poisson distribution around its value:\n\nusing Random, Distributions\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\ndata_fluctuated = Histogram(data.edges, Float64.(rand.(Poisson.(data.weights))))\n\ndata_hist = counts_to_histogram(get_counts(data_fluctuated, bin_edges), bin_edges)\nhists = get_best_fit_histograms(best, model, bin_edges)\n\nkeys(hists)\nKeySet for a OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, StatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1}} with 4 entries. Keys:\n  :total\n  :K40_hpge_bulk\n  :Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk","title":"Histograms of the best fit"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Grouping-the-sources","page":"Plotting","text":"A fit can have many sources, and group_histograms sums them into a few groups to plot fewer lines. A group is a label with the sources that go into it, one of\n\na list of source names, for example [:K40_hpge_bulk],\na regular expression that is matched to the names, for example r\"K40\",\n:rest, for all the sources that no other group selects.\n\nA source goes into the first group that selects it. The labels can be any text, including Unicode characters such as superscripts and Greek letters, and they are the names in the legend. The result has the same format as hists, with the total followed by the groups:\n\ngrouped = group_histograms(\n    hists,\n    [\"⁴⁰K\" => r\"K40\", \"²¹²Pb\" => [:Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk], \"other\" => :rest],\n)\n\nkeys(grouped)\nKeySet for a OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, StatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1}} with 4 entries. Keys:\n  :total\n  Symbol(\"⁴⁰K\")\n  Symbol(\"²¹²Pb\")\n  :other","title":"Grouping the sources"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plotting-the-best-fit","page":"Plotting","text":"The plot is made with a few functions that draw on a CairoMakie axis:\n\nfit_axes makes the figure, with an axis for the spectra and one for the residuals,\nplot_hist!(ax, h) draws the histogram h as a line, or filled with filled = true. The other keywords (color, linewidth, linestyle, label, ...) go to Makie, and a line without a color gets the next color of the palette,\nmark_blinded!(ax, h) draws the grey band over the empty bins of h,\nplot_residuals!(ax, prediction, data) draws the residuals in units of standard deviations, with the bands of 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations unless bands = false.\n\nAn example, with the data filled in light blue, the sources as colored lines and the best fit as a thicker black line, and with larger fonts:\n\nimport CairoMakie\n\n# larger fonts for the axes and the legends\nCairoMakie.update_theme!(\n    Axis = (xlabelsize = 26, ylabelsize = 26, xticklabelsize = 22, yticklabelsize = 22),\n    Legend = (labelsize = 22,),\n)\n\nfig, ax, ax_res = fit_axes(; ylabel = \"Counts per 10 keV\", size = (1300, 800))\n\n# the data, the groups of sources (with the colors of the palette) and the best fit\nplot_hist!(ax, data_hist; filled = true, color = :lightblue, label = \"Data\")\nfor (label, h) in grouped\n    label == :total || plot_hist!(ax, h; label = string(label))\nend\nplot_hist!(ax, grouped[:total]; color = :black, linewidth = 4, label = \"Best fit\")\n\n# the blinded region (the empty bins) and the residuals\nmark_blinded!(ax, grouped[:total])\nmark_blinded!(ax_res, grouped[:total])\nplot_residuals!(ax_res, grouped[:total], data_hist)\n\nCairoMakie.xlims!(ax, 500, 3200)\nCairoMakie.ylims!(ax, 1, 1e4)\nCairoMakie.ylims!(ax_res, -6, 6)\nCairoMakie.axislegend(ax; nbanks = 3)\nCairoMakie.axislegend(ax_res; nbanks = 3)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nnote: Note\nThe plot can be customised. The example is only a choice of style: the colors, line styles, line widths, labels and limits are keywords of plot_hist! and of the Makie functions on the axes, so any of them can be changed, and elements can be left out or added with other Makie functions. See the Makie documentation for the options.\n\nThe Figure can be saved with CairoMakie.save(\"fit.png\", fig). These functions call CairoMakie only with its full names, so loading both CairoMakie and Plots.jl with using makes names such as plot clash. Load one with import.","title":"Plotting the best fit"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Posterior-distributions","page":"Plotting","text":"The posterior samples from bat_sample are plotted with the plot recipes of BAT.jl, which use Plots.jl. See the plotting section of the BAT.jl manual. The tutorial shows them for a fit.","title":"Posterior distributions"},{"category":"section","location":"forward_model/#Forward-model","page":"Forward model","text":"The forward model is a function mapping the parameters of interest onto the predicted observables (in our case the counts in each bin).","title":"Forward model"},{"category":"section","location":"forward_model/#Definition","page":"Forward model","text":"In Refit.jl a linear template fit model is implemented, the predicted counts in energy bin i are a sum over the simulated spectra:\n\nmu_i(veca) = sum_k a_k times h_ki times N\n\nHere h_ki is the content of bin i in simulated spectrum k, N is the factor norm, and a_k is the parameter of spectrum k. Each simulated spectrum is scaled by its parameter and by norm, and the scaled spectra are added.\n\nIf norm holds the unit conversions and the livetime, a_k is the activity of source k. Usually the parameters are activities, and the model is written for that case. Any other choice of norm changes the meaning of a_k in the same way.\n\nbuild_forward_model turns a set of simulated energy spectra into such a function. This maps the activities to the predicted counts in each energy bin.","title":"Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"forward_model/#Building-the-model","page":"Forward model","text":"The examples in this manual use simplified spectra with three simulated sources, defined in example_data.jl. A real fit has many more sources. The simulated spectra are a dictionary of Histograms with the energy in keV, and all have the same binning:\n\n# the names of the simulated spectra, these name the parameters\nkeys(mc_dict)\nKeySet for a Dict{Symbol, StatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}} with 3 entries. Keys:\n  :K40_hpge_bulk\n  :Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk\n\n# keep the bins between 500 and 3200 keV\nmodel, bin_edges = build_forward_model(\n    mc_dict;\n    e_low = 500,\n    e_high = 3200,\n    norm = 1e-3 * livetime,  # mBq to Bq, times the livetime in seconds\n)\n\nmodel is the function mapping parameters to predicted counts, bin_edges describes its bins (see below).\n\nThe keys of mc_dict must be Symbols. They name the fit parameters. All histograms must have the same binning.\n\nKeyword Default Meaning\ne_low, e_high 500, 4000 Energy range in keV. Only bins fully inside are kept\nblind nothing (min, max) in keV. Bins overlapping this range are removed\nbinning 1 Number of consecutive bins merged before the range is applied\nnorm 1 Factor multiplying every spectrum, see below","title":"Building the model"},{"category":"section","location":"forward_model/#Calling-the-model","page":"Forward model","text":"The parameters are a flat named tuple with one activity per spectrum, named after the dictionary keys. Here the activities are in mBq, which norm converts to counts:\n\nparams = (\n    K40_hpge_bulk = 40.0,\n    Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk = 10.0,\n    two_vbb_hpge_bulk = 100.0,\n)\n\n# predicted counts in each bin\npred = model(params)\n270-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1559.0879642412722\n 1553.454045965951\n 1547.4651135047616\n 1541.137095370121\n 1534.4854325997348\n 1527.5288575667914\n 1522.5131150670659\n 1595.726061807834\n 1695.893074553892\n 1524.2000964583103\n    ⋮\n   64.12665169548922\n   63.246895185851415\n   62.37981837644299\n   61.52523966658751\n   60.68297989484833\n   59.852862311470666\n   59.03471255098211\n   58.22835860495515\n   57.433630794936576\n\npred is a flat vector with one entry per kept bin, here length(pred) = 270. Entry i is the sum over all spectra of activity * norm * (counts in the simulated bin). The order of the activities does not matter. A missing activity raises an error.","title":"Calling the model"},{"category":"section","location":"forward_model/#Bin-edges","page":"Forward model","text":"bin_edges holds the lower and upper edge in keV of each kept bin, in the same order as pred:\n\n# lower, upper edge of each bin in keV\nbin_edges.low, bin_edges.high\n([500.0, 510.0, 520.0, 530.0, 540.0, 550.0, 560.0, 570.0, 580.0, 590.0  …  3100.0, 3110.0, 3120.0, 3130.0, 3140.0, 3150.0, 3160.0, 3170.0, 3180.0, 3190.0], [510.0, 520.0, 530.0, 540.0, 550.0, 560.0, 570.0, 580.0, 590.0, 600.0  …  3110.0, 3120.0, 3130.0, 3140.0, 3150.0, 3160.0, 3170.0, 3180.0, 3190.0, 3200.0])\n\nWith blind the kept bins are not contiguous, so two vectors are returned instead of one edges vector.","title":"Bin edges"},{"category":"section","location":"forward_model/#Back-to-a-histogram","page":"Forward model","text":"counts_to_histogram turns the prediction into a Histogram, for example to plot the model at the best-fit parameters. It is the inverse of get_counts (see Likelihood).\n\n# the prediction as a Histogram, for plotting\nbest_fit = counts_to_histogram(pred, bin_edges)\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{Vector{Float64}}}\nedges:\n  [500.0, 510.0, 520.0, 530.0, 540.0, 550.0, 560.0, 570.0, 580.0, 590.0  …  3110.0, 3120.0, 3130.0, 3140.0, 3150.0, 3160.0, 3170.0, 3180.0, 3190.0, 3200.0]\nweights: [1559.0879642412722, 1553.454045965951, 1547.4651135047616, 1541.137095370121, 1534.4854325997348, 1527.5288575667914, 1522.5131150670659, 1595.726061807834, 1695.893074553892, 1524.2000964583103  …  65.01927197298386, 64.12665169548922, 63.246895185851415, 62.37981837644299, 61.52523966658751, 60.68297989484833, 59.852862311470666, 59.03471255098211, 58.22835860495515, 57.433630794936576]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false\n\nThe histogram covers the fit range, from bin_edges.low[1] to bin_edges.high[end]. A blinded region inside the range becomes one bin with 0 counts.\n\nSee the API for the full docstring.","title":"Back to a histogram"},{"category":"section","location":"priors/#Priors","page":"Priors","text":"get_priors guesses a range 0 .. a_max for the activity of each simulated spectrum from the measured spectrum.\n\nnote: Note\nThese are not real priors. They come from the data and only limit the range of the fit to improve convergence. Use external knowledge, for example a screening measurement, for informative priors.","title":"Priors"},{"category":"section","location":"priors/#The-maximum-activity","page":"Priors","text":"a_max is the largest activity for which the prediction does not exceed the data. In a window between low and high keV it is\n\na_mathrmmax = fracf  N_mathrmdatamathrmnorm  N_mathrmpdf\n\nwhere N_mathrmdata and N_mathrmpdf are the counts in the window of the data and of the simulated spectrum, norm is the factor of the forward model, and f is a safety factor. Only bins fully inside the window and outside the blinded region are used.\n\nTwo windows are used and the smaller a_max is kept:\n\nthe fit range [e_low, e_high], with the safety factor range_factor (default 1.1),\na reference range of the source, for example around a peak, with the safety factor peak_factor (default 1.5).","title":"The maximum activity"},{"category":"section","location":"priors/#Usage","page":"Priors","text":"# energy range in keV for each source, here around a peak\nref_ranges = Dict(\n    :K40 => (1456, 1466),\n    :Pb212_to_Pb208 => (2610, 2620),\n)\n\n# a range 0 .. a_max for the activity of each spectrum\npriors = get_priors(\n    mc_dict,\n    data;\n    ref_ranges,\n    e_low = 500,\n    e_high = 3200,\n    norm = 1e-3 * livetime,  # as in build_forward_model\n)\nDict{Symbol, Any} with 3 entries:\n  :K40_hpge_bulk            => 0.0 .. 203.524\n  :Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk => 0.0 .. 18.9729\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk        => 0.0 .. 153.192\n\nThe result is a Dict with the same keys as mc_dict. Each value is an interval 0 .. a_max.\n\nA spectrum uses the reference range of the longest key that its name starts with. For example :K40_hpge_bulk uses :K40. Spectra without a range only use the fit range. e_low, e_high, blind and norm are as in the forward model.","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"priors/#Changing-a-range-by-hand","page":"Priors","text":"The result is a dictionary, so single entries can be replaced:\n\nusing IntervalSets\n\n# replace the range of one spectrum by hand\npriors[:K40_hpge_bulk] = 0.0 .. 100.0\npriors\nDict{Symbol, Any} with 3 entries:\n  :K40_hpge_bulk            => 0.0 .. 100.0\n  :Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk => 0.0 .. 18.9729\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk        => 0.0 .. 153.192\n\nThe prior does not have to be a range. It can be any 1D distribution from Distributions.jl. For example, a Gaussian with mean 50 and width 10, truncated to positive activities between 0 and 1000.\n\nusing Distributions\n\npriors[:K40_hpge_bulk] = truncated(Normal(50.0, 10.0), 0.0, 1000.0)\npriors\nDict{Symbol, Any} with 3 entries:\n  :K40_hpge_bulk            => Truncated(Distributions.Normal{Float64}(μ=50.0, …\n  :Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk => 0.0 .. 18.9729\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk        => 0.0 .. 153.192","title":"Changing a range by hand"},{"category":"section","location":"priors/#The-prior-distribution","page":"Priors","text":"build_prior_dist turns the entries into a prior. A range becomes a uniform distribution on that range, a distribution is used as it is. rand(prior) draws one value for each activity as a named tuple, which goes directly into the forward model.\n\nprior = build_prior_dist(priors)\n\nrand(prior)\n(K40_hpge_bulk = 77.9930636305152, Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk = 7.327902563250425, two_vbb_hpge_bulk = 150.02208223906547)","title":"The prior distribution"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Saving-the-results","page":"Saving the results","text":"The results of a fit are saved for long term preservation and further analysis. The posterior samples, the best fit spectra as histograms in LH5 files, and the values of the parameters with their uncertainty in a YAML file. The tutorial shows all of them for a complete fit.","title":"Saving the results"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Posterior-samples","page":"Saving the results","text":"The samples are saved by BAT.jl, which writes them to an HDF5 file with bat_write and reads them back with bat_read. The package HDF5 has to be loaded:\n\nusing BAT, HDF5\n\nbat_write(\"samples.h5\", samples)\nsamples = bat_read(\"samples.h5\").result\n\nThe file has one dataset for each quantity, with one entry for each sample:\n\nv/<name>        value of each parameter (here the activities)\nweight          number of times the sample was repeated by the sampler\nlogd            logarithm of the posterior density at the sample\ninfo/chainid    chain, walker, step number and other bookkeeping of the sampler","title":"Posterior samples"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Best-fit-spectra","page":"Saving the results","text":"get_best_fit_histograms makes a histogram of the prediction of the model for the parameters, usually the best fit. The result is a dictionary with the total in the key :total and the contribution of each source, with the name of its activity. The bins outside the fit range and in the blinded region are empty.\n\nhists = get_best_fit_histograms(best, model, bin_edges)\n\nkeys(hists)\nKeySet for a OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, StatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1}} with 4 entries. Keys:\n  :total\n  :K40_hpge_bulk\n  :Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk\n\nwrite_best_fit writes them to an LH5 file, with the total in best_fit/total and each source in best_fit/<name>. With data the measured spectrum in the same bins is saved too, in data. All can be read back with read_hist.\n\nfile = joinpath(outdir, \"best_fit.lh5\")\n\ncounts = get_counts(data, bin_edges)\nwrite_best_fit(file, hists; data = counts_to_histogram(counts, bin_edges))\n\nread_hist(\"best_fit/K40_hpge_bulk\", file)\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{Vector{Float64}}}\nedges:\n  [500.0, 510.0, 520.0, 530.0, 540.0, 550.0, 560.0, 570.0, 580.0, 590.0  …  3110.0, 3120.0, 3130.0, 3140.0, 3150.0, 3160.0, 3170.0, 3180.0, 3190.0, 3200.0]\nweights: [380.099564729813, 375.37789210455037, 370.7148730386414, 366.1097789258701, 361.5618902109119, 357.0704962769026, 352.63489533440213, 348.25439431173896, 343.9283087467161, 339.6559626796624  …  14.738059519580842, 14.554980403895561, 14.37417553351072, 14.195616657297478, 14.019275875068153, 13.845125633216739, 13.673138720413649, 13.503288263353834, 13.335547722557822, 13.169890888224845]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false","title":"Best fit spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Parameter-values","page":"Saving the results","text":"write_parameter_values writes the values of the parameters, here the activities, to a YAML file. For each parameter it saves the best fit and an interval, and the file records the credible level of the intervals. The intervals are the smallest credible intervals: the shortest intervals that contain 68% of the posterior. BAT.jl computes them, and mode = :connected gives one interval for each parameter:\n\nusing Statistics\n\nbest = mode(samples)\nintervals = map(first, BAT.smallest_credible_intervals(samples; p = 0.68, mode = :connected))\n\nnote: Note\nBAT.jl marks smallest_credible_intervals as internal, so it may change between versions.\n\nHere the intervals are made up:\n\nusing IntervalSets\n\nfile = joinpath(outdir, \"parameters.yaml\")\n\nintervals = map(a -> 0.9a .. 1.1a, best)\nwrite_parameter_values(file, best, intervals; level = 0.68)\n\nprint(read(file, String))\ncredible_level: 0.68\nparameters:\n  K40_hpge_bulk:\n    best_fit: 40.0\n    low: 36.0\n    high: 44.0\n  Pb212_to_Pb208_hpge_bulk:\n    best_fit: 10.0\n    low: 9.0\n    high: 11.0\n  two_vbb_hpge_bulk:\n    best_fit: 100.0\n    low: 90.0\n    high: 110.00000000000001","title":"Parameter values"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Tutorial:-fitting-a-background-spectrum","page":"Tutorial","text":"This tutorial fits a measured energy spectrum with a sum of simulated spectra, from reading the file, sampling the posterior and plotting the results. This runs every time the documentation is built.\n\nThe fit uses automatically generated fake data (pseudo-experiments). The measured spectrum is generated from known activities, so the result of the fit can be compared with the truth at the end.","title":"Tutorial: fitting a background spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Fake-data","page":"Tutorial","text":"The input files are in test/test_files/tutorial of the repository, in LH5 format. They stand in for the output of a real simulation and for a measured spectrum. A real fit reads the files of the simulation instead. The script make_files.jl in the same directory makes them again.\n\nThere is one file for each simulated source, for example cables_K40.lh5, with the histograms pdf/mul1/icpc and pdf/mul1/bege (the two detector types) and the number of simulated primaries. The 2νββ decay has one file for each detector type.\ndata.lh5 has the measured histograms hist/mul1/icpc and hist/mul1/bege.\n\nThe sources with a γ line have a peak with a 1 keV sigma on a flat or linear continuum below the peak. The 2νββ spectrum is a broad Gaussian (600 keV width, centered at 1 MeV), and it makes about 70% of the counts. The ^39Ar spectrum is an exponential with a decay constant of 300 keV and makes about 20% of the counts. The measured spectrum is generated from known activities with Poisson fluctuations, so the result of the fit can be compared with the truth at the end.\n\ndir = \"../../test/test_files/tutorial\"  # relative to docs/src\nreaddir(dir)\n9-element Vector{String}:\n \"cables_K40.lh5\"\n \"cables_Pb212_to_Pb208.lh5\"\n \"data.lh5\"\n \"fibers_K40.lh5\"\n \"fibers_Pb212_to_Pb208.lh5\"\n \"hpge_bege_bulk_2vbb.lh5\"\n \"hpge_icpc_bulk_2vbb.lh5\"\n \"liquid_argon_Ar39.lh5\"\n \"make_files.jl\"","title":"Fake data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Settings","page":"Tutorial","text":"using Refit\nusing StatsBase\n\nlivetime = 1.0e6  # s\ne_low, e_high = 100, 4000  # keV, the range of the fit\nbinning = 20  # merge 20 bins of 1 keV into one\nblind = (2015.0, 2065.0)  # keV, a region around the 2039 keV Q-value that is not fitted\n\n# expected counts = norm * activity * spectrum, see the note on units below\nnorm = 1e-3 * livetime\n\n# the histograms of the measured spectrum and of each simulation are summed over these groups\nspec = [\"mul1/icpc\", \"mul1/bege\"]\n\nnote: Units\nIn the standard case, where the PDFs are normalized, the fit parameters represent the activity of the sources. The unit of the activity is the inverse of the unit of the livetime, or Bq if the livetime is in s. In many cases it is more convenient to use a different unit. For example, scaling norm by 10⁻³ changes the unit of the activity to mBq, as done here.","title":"Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Reading-the-data","page":"Tutorial","text":"read_hist reads one histogram. The data of the two detector types are added with add_histograms.\n\ndata = add_histograms([read_hist(\"hist/$s\", joinpath(dir, \"data.lh5\")) for s in spec])\nStatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}\nedges:\n  0.0:1.0:4000.0\nweights: [2546.0, 2442.0, 2526.0, 2492.0, 2494.0, 2438.0, 2409.0, 2494.0, 2467.0, 2519.0  …  2.0, 4.0, 4.0, 2.0, 0.0, 3.0, 2.0, 4.0, 1.0, 2.0]\nclosed: left\nisdensity: false","title":"Reading the data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Reading-the-simulated-spectra","page":"Tutorial","text":"Each source has its own file. read_pdf sums the groups and divides by the number of simulated primaries, so that the spectrum is in counts per primary. The name of each spectrum is a Symbol, the activity of the source gets the same name.\n\ngroups = [\"pdf/$s\" for s in spec]\n\ncomponents = [\"cables\", \"fibers\"]\nisotopes = [\"K40\", \"Pb212_to_Pb208\"]\n\nmc_dict = Dict(\n    Symbol(comp, \"_\", iso) => read_pdf(joinpath(dir, \"$(comp)_$iso.lh5\"); groups) for\n    comp in components for iso in isotopes\n)\nkeys(mc_dict)\nKeySet for a Dict{Symbol, StatsBase.Histogram{Float64, 1, Tuple{StepRangeLen{Float64, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Base.TwicePrecision{Float64}, Int64}}}} with 4 entries. Keys:\n  :fibers_Pb212_to_Pb208\n  :cables_K40\n  :fibers_K40\n  :cables_Pb212_to_Pb208\n\nThe 2νββ decay is simulated once for each detector type. read_pdf accepts a dictionary from file name to weight and returns the weighted average of the spectra. The weights are normalised. Here the weight is the mass of each detector type in kg.\n\nmass = Dict(\"icpc\" => 80.0, \"bege\" => 20.0)\nbulk = Dict(joinpath(dir, \"hpge_$(t)_bulk_2vbb.lh5\") => w for (t, w) in mass)\n\nmc_dict[:two_vbb_hpge_bulk] = read_pdf(bulk; groups)\n\nThe ^39Ar decay has one file.\n\nmc_dict[:Ar39_liquid_argon] = read_pdf(joinpath(dir, \"liquid_argon_Ar39.lh5\"); groups)\n\nAlpha events have no simulation. As a simple model they are a constant spectrum. Its activity is the number of counts in each 1 keV bin, so the spectrum is 1 / norm in every bin.\n\nmc_dict[:alpha] = Histogram(data.edges, fill(1 / norm, length(data.weights)))","title":"Reading the simulated spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Forward-model-and-likelihood","page":"Tutorial","text":"The forward model maps the activities to the expected counts in each energy bin. The likelihood compares these with the measured counts, which are taken from the data in the same bins.\n\nmodel, bin_edges = build_forward_model(mc_dict; e_low, e_high, blind, binning, norm)\ncounts = get_counts(data, bin_edges)\nlikelihood = build_likelihood(counts, model)","title":"Forward model and likelihood"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Ranges-for-the-activities","page":"Tutorial","text":"The fit needs a range for each activity. get_priors estimates it from the data (see Priors). The reference ranges give the energy window of a peak of each isotope in keV. The 1461 keV line of ^40K and the 2615 keV line of ^208Tl limit the activities of these sources.\n\nref_ranges = Dict(:K40 => (1456, 1466), :Pb212_to_Pb208 => (2610, 2620))\n\nranges = get_priors(mc_dict, data; ref_ranges, e_low, e_high, blind, norm)\nDict{Symbol, Any} with 7 entries:\n  :fibers_Pb212_to_Pb208 => 0.0 .. 5186.73\n  :alpha                 => 0.0 .. 770.385\n  :cables_K40            => 0.0 .. 5267.81\n  :fibers_K40            => 0.0 .. 5162.57\n  :cables_Pb212_to_Pb208 => 0.0 .. 5255.73\n  :Ar39_liquid_argon     => 0.0 .. 6900.73\n  :two_vbb_hpge_bulk     => 0.0 .. 5081.74\n\nA range can be changed by hand where the guess is poor, for example ranges[:two_vbb_hpge_bulk] = 0.0 .. 20.0 (with using IntervalSets).\n\nbuild_prior_dist makes the prior: a uniform distribution on the range of each activity. rand(prior) draws one random value for each activity, a named tuple that goes directly into the model.\n\nprior = build_prior_dist(ranges)\n\nrand(prior)\n(fibers_Pb212_to_Pb208 = 2410.4868635070607, alpha = 30.301025181788702, cables_K40 = 1441.5466908930168, fibers_K40 = 3571.56660581935, cables_Pb212_to_Pb208 = 3943.408692025786, Ar39_liquid_argon = 3707.3611298275414, two_vbb_hpge_bulk = 1270.2663182119613)","title":"Ranges for the activities"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Sampling-the-posterior","page":"Tutorial","text":"BAT.jl samples the posterior with a Markov chain Monte Carlo.\n\nusing BAT\n\nposterior = PosteriorMeasure(likelihood, prior)\n\nsamples = bat_sample(\n    posterior,\n    TransformedMCMC(\n        proposal = RandomWalk(),\n        strict = false,\n        burnin = MCMCMultiCycleBurnin(nsteps_per_cycle = 50_000, max_ncycles = 50),\n        nsteps = 5 * 10^5,\n        nchains = 4,\n    ),\n).result\n[ Info: MCMCChainPoolInit: trying to generate 4 viable MCMC chain state(s).\n[ Info: Selected 4 MCMC chain state(s).\n[ Info: Begin tuning of 4 MCMC chain(s).\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 1 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 0 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 2 finished, 4 chains, 3 tuned, 0 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 3 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 0 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 4 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 0 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 5 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 0 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 6 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 0 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC Tuning cycle 7 finished, 4 chains, 4 tuned, 4 converged.\n[ Info: MCMC tuning of 4 chains successful after 7 cycle(s).\n[ Info: Running post-tuning stabilization steps for 4 MCMC chain(s).\n[ Info: Generate main samples using 4 MCMC chain(s).","title":"Sampling the posterior"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Result","page":"Tutorial","text":"The activities used to make the fake data are:\n\ntrue_activities = (  # mBq\n    cables_K40 = 200.0,\n    fibers_K40 = 100.0,\n    cables_Pb212_to_Pb208 = 50.0,\n    fibers_Pb212_to_Pb208 = 30.0,\n    two_vbb_hpge_bulk = 3500.0,\n    Ar39_liquid_argon = 1000.0,\n)\ntrue_alpha = 2.0  # counts per 1 keV bin\n\nThe marginal posterior of each activity is plotted with the plot recipes of BAT.jl. The colored regions are the smallest intervals that contain 68%, 95% and 99.7% of the distribution, the dashed line is the mode and the red line is the true activity used to make the measured spectrum.\n\nusing Plots\n\ntruth = merge(true_activities, (alpha = true_alpha,))\npanels = map(collect(keys(truth))) do name\n    unit = name == :alpha ? \"counts per keV\" : \"mBq\"\n    plot(\n        samples,\n        name;\n        mean = false,\n        std = false,\n        globalmode = true,\n        nbins = 50,\n        title = string(name),\n        titlefontsize = 8,\n        xlabel = \"activity ($unit)\",\n        ylabel = \"probability density (1/$unit)\",\n    )\n    vline!([truth[name]]; color = :red, linewidth = 2, label = \"\")\nend\nplot(\n    panels...;\n    layout = (2, 4),\n    size = (1300, 700),\n    legend = false,\n    left_margin = 8Plots.mm,\n    bottom_margin = 6Plots.mm,\n    top_margin = 3Plots.mm,\n)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=49}\n\nA pair of activities shows their correlation. The ^40K activities of the cables and of the fibers are anticorrelated, because the data constrain mostly their sum:\n\nplot(\n    samples,\n    (:cables_K40, :fibers_K40);\n    nbins = 50,\n    globalmode = true,\n    legend = :bottomleft,\n    xlabel = \"cables_K40 activity (mBq)\",\n    ylabel = \"fibers_K40 activity (mBq)\",\n)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=8}\n\nThe best fit is the global mode. The uncertainty of each activity is the smallest 68% credible interval: the smallest interval that contains 68% of its posterior density. BAT.jl computes it for all the activities at once, and mode = :connected gives a single interval for each one. Activities differ by orders of magnitude, so the plot shows the best fit divided by the true value. A good fit is consistent with 1.\n\nbest = mode(samples)\nintervals = map(first, BAT.smallest_credible_intervals(samples; p = 0.68, mode = :connected))\n\nnames = collect(keys(truth))\nfit = [best[n] / truth[n] for n in names]\nlower = [intervals[n].left / truth[n] for n in names]\nupper = [intervals[n].right / truth[n] for n in names]\n\nscatter(\n    string.(names),\n    fit;\n    yerror = (max.(fit .- lower, 0), max.(upper .- fit, 0)),\n    color = :black,\n    label = \"best fit and 68% interval\",\n    ylabel = \"best fit / true activity\",\n    xrotation = 30,\n    bottom_margin = 12Plots.mm,\n    legend = :bottomright,\n)\nhline!([1]; color = :red, label = \"true activity\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nFinally, the prediction of the best fit is compared with the measured counts. The sources are summed into a few groups and plotted with CairoMakie, here in the LEGEND style of LegendMakie.jl. See Plotting for the functions that are used and how the plot can be customised.\n\nusing LegendMakie\nimport Format\n\nimport CairoMakie\n\ndata_hist = counts_to_histogram(counts, bin_edges)\nhists = get_best_fit_histograms(best, model, bin_edges)\n\n# sum the sources into a few lines\ngrouped = group_histograms(\n    hists,\n    [\n        \"⁴⁰K\" => r\"K40\",\n        \"²¹²Pb → ²⁰⁸Pb\" => r\"Pb212\",\n        \"2νββ\" => r\"two_vbb\",\n        \"³⁹Ar\" => r\"Ar39\",\n        \"other\" => :rest,\n    ],\n)\n\nfig, ax, ax_res = fit_axes(; ylabel = \"Counts per $binning keV\")\n\n# the data, the groups of sources (with the colors of the palette) and the best fit\nplot_hist!(ax, data_hist; filled = true, alpha = 0.6, color = :lightblue, label = \"Data\")\nfor (label, h) in grouped\n    label == :total || plot_hist!(ax, h; label = string(label))\nend\nplot_hist!(ax, grouped[:total]; color = :black, linewidth = 4, label = \"Best fit\")\n\n# the blinded region (the empty bins) and the residuals\nmark_blinded!(ax, grouped[:total])\nmark_blinded!(ax_res, grouped[:total])\nplot_residuals!(ax_res, grouped[:total], data_hist)\n\nCairoMakie.xlims!(ax, e_low, e_high)\nCairoMakie.ylims!(ax, 1, 1e5)\nCairoMakie.ylims!(ax_res, -6, 6)\nCairoMakie.axislegend(ax; nbanks = 3)\nCairoMakie.axislegend(ax_res; nbanks = 3)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Result"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Saving-the-results","page":"Tutorial","text":"The results are saved in formats for long term preservation (see Saving the results). The posterior samples are written by BAT.jl to an HDF5 file, which needs the package HDF5. The best fit histograms, of the total and of each source, and the measured spectrum go into an LH5 file. The values of the activities with the smallest 68% interval go into a YAML file.\n\nusing HDF5\n\noutdir = mktempdir()\n\nbat_write(joinpath(outdir, \"samples.h5\"), samples)\nwrite_best_fit(joinpath(outdir, \"best_fit.lh5\"), hists; data = data_hist)\nwrite_parameter_values(joinpath(outdir, \"parameters.yaml\"), best, intervals; level = 0.68)\n\nreaddir(outdir)\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"best_fit.lh5\"\n \"parameters.yaml\"\n \"samples.h5\"\n\nThe YAML file is plain text:\n\nprint(read(joinpath(outdir, \"parameters.yaml\"), String))\ncredible_level: 0.68\nparameters:\n  fibers_Pb212_to_Pb208:\n    best_fit: 34.864901595725335\n    low: 29.446169684546735\n    high: 40.561064884573746\n  alpha:\n    best_fit: 2.009154720134364\n    low: 1.9686180329142435\n    high: 2.066186950336854\n  cables_K40:\n    best_fit: 195.76895981350094\n    low: 164.82529241851418\n    high: 223.4964743106147\n  fibers_K40:\n    best_fit: 102.25959008084138\n    low: 76.1630509840067\n    high: 132.39559400988526\n  cables_Pb212_to_Pb208:\n    best_fit: 44.26229783827031\n    low: 38.315022698213085\n    high: 50.67743806103673\n  Ar39_liquid_argon:\n    best_fit: 1004.6143431456771\n    low: 1000.0549478179196\n    high: 1009.4274987901757\n  two_vbb_hpge_bulk:\n    best_fit: 3499.338390157033\n    low: 3494.541477942581\n    high: 3503.960086461518\n\nThe saved samples and histograms read back as they were written:\n\nsamples_saved = bat_read(joinpath(outdir, \"samples.h5\")).result\nfit_saved = read_hist(\"best_fit/total\", joinpath(outdir, \"best_fit.lh5\"))\n\n(length(samples_saved) == length(samples), fit_saved.weights == hists[:total].weights)\n(true, true)","title":"Saving the results"},{"category":"section","location":"#Refit.jl","page":"Home","text":"Tools to perform Bayesian (with BAT.jl fits of binned data with models based on Monte-Carlo simulations, often generated with remage.\n\nRefit.jl is a toolkit, it does not provide an end-to-end fitting tool. A fit compares a measured energy spectrum with a sum of simulated spectra. Each simulated spectrum is scaled by the activity. The parameters are found by sampling the posterior, which combines the likelihood of the data with priors.","title":"Refit.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#User-manual","page":"Home","text":"Refit.jl can also do a lot more than this, for details see the following pages!\n\nReading histograms: read the data and the simulated spectra and normalise them\nForward model: map the activities to the predicted counts in each energy bin\nLikelihood: compare the predicted counts with the measured ones\nPriors: estimate ranges for the activities from the data\nPlotting: plot the best fit, make plots with a different style, and plot the posteriors\nSaving the results: save the samples, the best fit spectra and the activities","title":"User manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tutorial","page":"Home","text":"Fitting a background spectrum: a complete fit on made-up data, from reading the files to sampling the posterior","title":"Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"API: docstrings of all functions","title":"Reference"}]
}
